ote`, `mi.info`, and `t.production_year` have been combined into a single AND condition using logical operators. This simplification has likely improved the performance of the query by reducing the number of conditions that need to be evaluated.\n\nIn summary, the query rewrite rule\'s Case 3 has been applied to simplify the WHERE clause conditions in the original query, resulting in a rewritten query with reduced complexity and redundancy."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 2:\n"""According to the query rewrite rule, Case 1 transforms the query as follows:\n\n- Directly apply the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before the projection.\n- Eliminate the outer projection layer if it only renames the columns without transforming them.\n\nIn this case, the original query has been rewritten with a new `LogicalAggregate` node that applies the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions directly to the data source. The outer projection layer has been eliminated because it only renames the columns without transforming them."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\n"""The rewritten logical plan shows that the conditions in the WHERE clause have been moved into the ON clause of the INNER JOIN. For example, the condition `<>($2(country_code), \'[us]\')` has been moved from the WHERE clause to the ON clause of the first LogicalJoin operation. Similarly, the condition `=($10(info), \'countries\')` has been moved from the WHERE clause to a LogicalFilter operation within the subquery. This transformation is in accordance with Case 1 of the query rewrite rule.\n\nThe other conditions in the WHERE clause have also been transformed accordingly, moving them into the ON clause or as a WHERE clause on a subquery of the respective side."""'}], 'response': "Here's a summary of the provided SQL query rewrite strategies:\n\nThe original SQL query has been rewritten to simplify its complexity and redundancy. The first strategy removed unnecessary conditions in